# Cursor AI Rules for auto-code-completion

## Project Overview
This is a Next.js 16 project with TypeScript, React 19, and Tailwind CSS 4.
Focus on type safety, modern React patterns, and Next.js App Router conventions.

## Core Principles
1. Type Safety First - Always use TypeScript with strict mode
2. Server Components by Default - Use 'use client' only when necessary
3. Performance Matters - Optimize bundle size and runtime performance
4. Accessibility Always - Follow WCAG 2.1 AA standards
5. Security by Design - Validate inputs, sanitize outputs, prevent XSS/injection

## TypeScript Guidelines
- Use `interface` for object shapes, `type` for unions/intersections
- Avoid `any` - use `unknown` if type is truly unknown
- Leverage TypeScript utility types (Pick, Omit, Partial, etc.)
- Define prop interfaces for all components
- Use proper return types for functions
- Enable strict null checks

## React Best Practices
- Prefer functional components with hooks
- Use Server Components for data fetching
- Add 'use client' directive only for:
  - useState, useEffect, other client hooks
  - Event handlers (onClick, onChange, etc.)
  - Browser APIs (localStorage, window, etc.)
- Implement error boundaries for error handling
- Use React.memo() for expensive computations
- Avoid prop drilling - use context or composition

## Next.js Patterns
- Use App Router (app/ directory)
- Server Actions for mutations
- Dynamic imports for code splitting
- Next.js Image component for images
- Metadata API for SEO
- Route handlers for API endpoints
- Loading and error UI patterns

## Component Structure
```typescript
// Server Component (default)
interface ComponentProps {
  id: string;
  children?: React.ReactNode;
}

export function Component({ id, children }: ComponentProps) {
  // Async data fetching allowed here
  return <div>{children}</div>;
}
```

```typescript
// Client Component (when needed)
'use client'

import { useState } from 'react';

interface ClientComponentProps {
  initialValue: string;
}

export function ClientComponent({ initialValue }: ClientComponentProps) {
  const [value, setValue] = useState(initialValue);
  return <button onClick={() => setValue('new')}>{value}</button>;
}
```

## Styling Guidelines
- Use Tailwind utility classes
- Follow mobile-first responsive design
- Use semantic color names from Tailwind
- Group related classes: layout, spacing, colors, typography
- Extract repeated patterns into components
- Use Tailwind's dark mode classes when applicable

## File Organization
```
app/
  ├── (routes)/          # Route groups
  ├── api/               # API routes
  ├── components/        # Shared components
  └── layout.tsx         # Root layout

lib/
  ├── utils/             # Utility functions
  ├── hooks/             # Custom React hooks
  ├── types/             # Shared TypeScript types
  └── constants/         # Application constants
```

## Naming Conventions
- Components: PascalCase (UserProfile.tsx)
- Files: kebab-case or PascalCase matching component name
- Functions/Variables: camelCase (getUserData)
- Constants: UPPER_SNAKE_CASE (API_URL)
- Types/Interfaces: PascalCase (UserData)
- CSS classes: Tailwind utilities only

## Error Handling
- Use try-catch for async operations
- Implement error boundaries for UI errors
- Provide meaningful error messages
- Log errors appropriately
- Handle edge cases explicitly
- Validate user inputs

## Security
- Sanitize all user inputs
- Use environment variables for secrets
- Implement CSRF protection for forms
- Validate data on server side
- Use Content Security Policy headers
- Prevent XSS with proper escaping
- Avoid eval() and dangerous HTML insertion

## Performance
- Use Server Components to reduce client JS
- Implement lazy loading for heavy components
- Optimize images (next/image)
- Use proper caching strategies
- Minimize re-renders with React.memo
- Code split with dynamic imports
- Prefetch critical resources

## Code Quality
- Write self-documenting code
- Add comments only when necessary (explain "why", not "what")
- Keep functions small and focused
- Follow single responsibility principle
- Use meaningful variable names
- Avoid magic numbers/strings
- DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself)

## Testing Expectations
- Write testable code
- Separate business logic from UI
- Use dependency injection when needed
- Mock external dependencies
- Test edge cases and error states

## Git Commit Guidelines
- Use conventional commits format
- Keep commits atomic and focused
- Write clear, descriptive commit messages
- Reference issues when applicable

## Don't Do This
❌ Using 'any' type
❌ Mixing client and server code incorrectly
❌ Inline styles instead of Tailwind
❌ Prop drilling more than 2 levels
❌ Large components (>200 lines)
❌ Ignoring TypeScript errors
❌ Using var instead of const/let
❌ Mutating state directly
❌ Forgetting to handle loading/error states
❌ Not validating user inputs

## Do This Instead
✅ Use specific types or unknown
✅ Separate client/server components clearly
✅ Use Tailwind utility classes
✅ Use context or composition
✅ Extract smaller components
✅ Fix TypeScript errors properly
✅ Use const by default, let when needed
✅ Use setState or immutable updates
✅ Always handle loading/error states
✅ Validate and sanitize inputs

## When Implementing Features
1. Understand the requirement fully
2. Check existing patterns in the codebase
3. Plan the component structure
4. Implement with type safety
5. Handle errors and edge cases
6. Optimize for performance
7. Test the implementation
8. Document if complex

## When Debugging
1. Check TypeScript errors first
2. Review React DevTools
3. Check browser console
4. Verify data flow
5. Check network requests
6. Review server logs
7. Isolate the issue
8. Fix root cause, not symptoms

## Useful Commands
- `npm run dev` - Start development server
- `npm run build` - Build for production
- `npm run lint` - Run ESLint
- `npm run start` - Start production server

